title:: MIRa MS-20 ESP analysis
summary:: Normalized F/V, envelope, trigger and event-timing analysis
categories:: Guides>Analysis
related:: Classes/FreqToVolt, Classes/Periodicity2, Classes/EnvFollow2, Classes/TriggerDetector, Classes/Periodicity, Classes/PitchMPM

description::
The four new analyzers expose only finite signals in [0,1]. FreqToVolt counts conditioned crossings; its frequency indication can disagree with fundamental pitch on harmonically complex input. Periodicity2 measures same-direction event timing. Existing Periodicity uses correlation evidence and remains a separate analyzer.

section:: Modes and calibration
FreqToVolt has exactly two literal modes: code::\digital:: and code::\circuit::. Mode and all numeric parameters are fixed at Synth construction; recreate the Synth to change them. Audio input is processed sample by sample; the public outputs update once per control block. TriggerDetector accepts a control-rate normalized envelope and emits a one-block activation pulse.

Digital F/V uses unit-area crossing impulses and exponential averaging. Circuit F/V uses second-order high-pass, fourth-order low-pass, an idealized comparator and retriggerable pulse, followed by second-order smoothing coupled to LOW CUT. The original service drawing has an unresolved final LM339 connection; timing width and voltage gain are explicit software approximations. This is a topology-informed reduced model with numerical validation, not measured MS-20 hardware equivalence. The preamp is replaced by a calibrated amplified-branch input: one audio unit after inputGain equals one model volt, with assumed headroom limits. Physical sound-card voltages are not calibrated by this convention.

Circuit highCut specifies the first low-pass section's natural frequency; lowCut specifies the high-pass natural frequency. Component-derived resistance limits may constrain requests. Bilinear discretization warps upper-band response. Circuit output smoothing follows lowCut; smoothTime affects only digital mode. pulseWidth affects only circuit mode. Digital filter cutoff zero bypasses; circuit stages are always present.

The nominal internal reference is 8.4 V for F/V and 5 V for ENV. Public values are unitless. For unclipped F/V frequency output, indicated Hz equals code::frequency * referenceMaxFreq::; event rate similarly uses referenceMaxEventRate. The software voltage calibration also depends on referenceMaxFreq. Clipping at one loses information. The circuit validity flag clears for recent pulse overlap or clipped indication; event evidence never proves a unique F0. MS-20 Hz/V pitch control is proportional in voltage to frequency: doubling voltage nominally doubles pitch frequency, unlike a 1 V/oct exponential pitch input. These normalized outlets are neither hardware voltage nor ready-to-send 1 V/oct CV.

EnvFollow2 circuit mode is a half-wave storage detector with a fixed diode approximation, approximately 0.96 ms charging and 22 ms release. Digital mode uses absolute-value attack/release smoothing and shares the declared volts/reference convention. attackTime/releaseTime affect only digital mode. TriggerDetector uses the normalized envelope; both modes use the approved reduced Schmitt law, without a measured threshold knob taper. hardwareGate is one when inactive and zero when active. TriggerDetector cannot recover envelope information already clipped at one.

Periodicity2 needs minIntervals complete intervals. Count support reaches one at twice that count; freshness decays after one expected interval toward zero at timeout. Its 128-interval capacity can shorten the effective history at high rates. First useful latency therefore depends on event rate, windowTime and minIntervals; filter and smoothing latency are additional. Stable wrong crossings can score highly.

section:: Digital and circuit comparison
code::
(
{
    var input=SinOsc.ar(220,0,4);
    var digital=FreqToVolt.kr(input,mode:\digital);
    var circuit=FreqToVolt.kr(input,mode:\circuit);
    digital.poll(2,"digital: cv/frequency/eventRate/valid");
    circuit.poll(2,"circuit: cv/frequency/eventRate/valid");
    Silent.ar(2)
}.play;
)
::

section:: Audio-controlled musical patch
Start with a low monitoring level. Adjust the explicit input gain for a guitar or microphone source.
code::
(
{
    var input=SoundIn.ar(0)*4;
    var fv=FreqToVolt.kr(input,mode:\circuit);
    var env=EnvFollow2.kr(input);
    var detected=TriggerDetector.kr(env,threshold:0.1);
    var note=Decay2.kr(detected[0],0.005,0.15);
    var hz=(fv[1]*2000).clip(40,2000);
    var sound=SinOsc.ar(hz)*env*(0.25+(note*0.75))*detected[1]*0.15;
    sound!2
}.play;
)
::

section:: Timing versus pitch comparison
code::
(
{
    var input=SinOsc.ar(220,0,4);
    var fv=FreqToVolt.kr(input);
    var timing=Periodicity2.kr(input);
    var correlation=Periodicity.kr(input);
    var pitch=PitchMPM.kr(input);
    (fv++timing++[correlation]++pitch).poll(2,"ESP / timing / existing correlation / MPM");
    Silent.ar(2)
}.play;
)
::

section:: Validation limits
See code::docs/ms20-esp/validation.md:: and code::circuit-assumptions.md:: in the source distribution for equations, test coverage, calibration and unresolved electrical details. No SPICE comparison or instrument measurements have been performed. The optional Supernova build requires a complete matching SuperCollider source tree including nova-tt.
